  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nSurface high pressure remains in control the next couple of days\nbefore our next system midweek. Cannot rule out some fog development\ntonight, particularly near the river valleys. \n\nAn occluding low pressure system will scoot across the northern \nGreat Lakes midweek, eventually sweeping a cold front through the \nOhio Valley. Temperatures will begin to warm back into the lower to \nmiddle 80s on Wednesday, but the warm sector won't be all that \nimpressive. PWATs only appear to increase to around to around 1.25\" \nahead of the cold front, which isn't all that high for the month of \nAugust. As such, ensembles aren't showing a heavy rainfall footprint \nfrom this system. Additionally, it does appear that the ILN CWA will \nbe out of phase with the better chances for stronger convection \nunless the front slows down a bit, which could result in more \nthunderstorm activity for portions of central OH down into north- \ncentral KY on Thursday. Nonetheless, still not overly concerned about\na severe component with this system, but will have to monitor timing\ntrends.",
